<article class="env-lemma" id="0CNX">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0CNX">Lemma <span data-tag="0CNX">37.48.3</span>.</a> Let $S$ be a scheme. If $U \subset S$ is open and $V \to U$ is a surjective integral morphism, then there exists a surjective integral morphism $\overline{V} \to S$ with $\overline{V} \times _ S U$ isomorphic to $V$ as schemes over $U$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Let $V' \to S$ be the normalization of $S$ in $U$, see Morphisms, Section <a href="/tag/0BAK" data-tag="0BAK">29.54</a>. By construction $V' \to S$ is integral. By Morphisms, Lemmas <a href="/tag/035K" data-tag="035K">29.54.6</a> and <a href="/tag/03GP" data-tag="03GP">29.54.12</a> we see that the inverse image of $U$ in $V'$ is $V$. Let $Z$ be the reduced induced scheme structure on $S \setminus U$. Then $\overline{V} = V' \amalg Z$ works. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>